No, do not create a new file. You should modify your existing GlobalExceptionHandler.java.
The error happened because your current file has two different methods trying to handle the same thing (DataAccessException). Spring gets "confused" when it sees duplicates during startup. To fix this, you just need to merge them into one.
The Fix
 * Open your existing GlobalExceptionHandler.java.
 * Find and delete both handleDbError and handleDatabaseSecurity.
 * Paste this single, combined method in their place:
/**
 * Rule 2 & 3: Consolidated Database Exception Handler.
 * This resolves the 'Ambiguous @ExceptionHandler' error by 
 * combining previous database handlers into one secure method.
 */
@ExceptionHandler(org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException.class)
public ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponseDto> handleDatabaseOperation(
        org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException ex, 
        jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest request) {
    
    // 1. Extract the root ORA- code from Oracle
    Throwable rootCause = org.springframework.core.NestedExceptionUtils.getMostSpecificCause(ex);
    String errorCode = "DB_TECHNICAL_ERROR";
    
    if (rootCause instanceof java.sql.SQLException) {
        // Rule 2: Log only the Error Code (PII Protection)
        errorCode = "ORA-" + ((java.sql.SQLException) rootCause).getErrorCode();
    }

    // Rule 2: Sanitize logs by masking raw SQL error messages
    log.error("DATABASE_FAULT | Path: {} | ErrorCode: {} | Status: Masked", 
              request.getRequestURI(), errorCode);

    // 2. Return a clean, safe response to the frontend
    return buildResponse(
        HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR, 
        "Database Error", 
        "A technical error occurred in the data layer. Reference: " + errorCode, 
        request.getRequestURI(), 
        null
    );
}

Why this works:
By having only one @ExceptionHandler for DataAccessException, you remove the ambiguity that was causing the BeanCreationException. Your application will now be able to start the context properly because the "conflict" is gone.
